\title{\textbf{IMU Yoga Repetition Quality — Binary Classification}}
\author{}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\textbf{Adilkhan} and \textbf{Darkhan} are young researchers who decided to explore how technology can improve physical training.  
They equipped participants with \textbf{IMU sensors} and recorded multiple yoga pose repetitions.  
Each attempt was carefully labeled as either \texttt{correct} or \texttt{incorrect}.  

Their goal is simple: to train a model that can automatically recognize whether a repetition is done properly.  
Now, it is your mission to continue their work and build an accurate classifier to evaluate unseen yoga repetitions.

\section{Formal Problem Statement}

You are given three CSV files:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{X\_train.csv}: Training set with IMU data segments.  
    \item \texttt{y\_train.csv}: Labels for the training set (\texttt{0} = incorrect, \texttt{1} = correct).  
    \item \texttt{X\_test.csv}: Test set with IMU data segments, without labels. Your model must predict the label for each test example.
\end{itemize}

The dataset can be accessed here:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \href{https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wMuHEYsXKt3_IhgAUiUcpPvuctLM9jGL/view?usp=drive_link}{\textbf{X\_train.csv}}
    \item \href{https://drive.google.com/file/d/1F3mUFseTH3mi6bkY13flJTAK4tGTMIzm/view?usp=drive_link}{\textbf{y\_train.csv}}
    \item \href{https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OdlLTsLPyLa7JUmhQcRtP9ixlYE82H7k/view?usp=drive_link}{\textbf{X\_test.csv}}
\end{itemize}

\section{Input}

\subsection{Features (X)}
Each repetition is represented by time series of IMU readings sampled at \textbf{200 Hz}. The following sensor signals are provided:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{ax}, \texttt{ay}, \texttt{az} — Linear acceleration (g) along the sensor’s X, Y, Z axes.
    \item \texttt{wx}, \texttt{wy}, \texttt{wz} — Angular velocity (deg/s) around the sensor’s X, Y, Z axes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Labels (y)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{0} — repetition performed incorrectly.
    \item \texttt{1} — repetition performed correctly.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
Each \texttt{id} corresponds to one yoga repetition.

\section{Output}

Participants must submit only a CSV file named \textbf{solution.csv}, with the following format:
\begin{verbatim}
id,label
0,1
1,0
2,1
...
\end{verbatim}

\noindent
\textbf{Important:} Submit only the \texttt{solution.csv} file. Source code is not required. The order of \texttt{id} values in \texttt{solution.csv} must strictly follow the order in \texttt{test.csv}, starting from \texttt{0} up to the highest ID.

\section{Evaluation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Your predictions will be evaluated using the \textbf{Accuracy score}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Example}

\noindent
\textbf{Input (\texttt{X\_test.csv}):}
\begin{verbatim}
id,ax,ay,az,wx,wy,wz
0,0.12,0.04,0.98,-1.2,0.3,0.1
1,0.01,0.15,1.02,0.8,-0.5,0.0
2,-0.10,0.20,0.95,0.7,0.1,-0.2
\end{verbatim}

\noindent
\textbf{Your Output (\texttt{solution.csv}):}
\begin{verbatim}
id,label
0,1
1,0
2,1
\end{verbatim}

\end{document}

